    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Kemalism is the ideology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> in 1923, summed up in the six principles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-six-arrows">Six Arrows</a>: republicanism, populism, nationalism, laicism, statism and reformism.</li><li>Its reforms abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in 1924, replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> with an adapted Swiss Civil Code in 1926 and struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> from the constitution as the state religion in 1928, and in 1937 the six principles became state ideology.</li><li>It became a model for modernizers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, while its nationalism denied the identity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> for decades and treated non-Muslims as second-class citizens.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Under the Kemalist reforms Turks had to adopt surnames, the weekly day of rest moved from Friday to Sunday, and wearing the fez, which Atatürk saw as a tie to the Ottoman past, was penalized.</p></aside>
    </section>

