    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1908</span><span class="rf-text">The Young Turk Revolution reinstates the Ottoman constitution; Atatürk is a member of the Committee of Union and Progress</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1923</span><span class="rf-text">Atatürk founds the Republican People’s Party, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> is declared</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text">The Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> is abolished, and the office of Shaykh al-Islām is replaced by the Presidency of Religious Affairs</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1926</span><span class="rf-text">The mejelle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> codes give way to an adapted Swiss Civil Code and a penal code on German and Italian models</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1928</span><span class="rf-text">The article naming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as the established religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> is removed from the constitution</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1929</span><span class="rf-text">Yakup Kadri Karaosmanoğlu uses the word Kemalism for the principles of the Turkish path to modernity</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1937</span><span class="rf-text">The six principles are written into the constitution as state ideology</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1982</span><span class="rf-text">A new constitution after the military coup of 1980 declares the Republic loyal to the nationalism of Atatürk</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence.jpg" alt="Turkish War of Independence" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C3%BCrk_Kurtulu%C5%9F_Sava%C5%9F%C4%B1_-_kolaj.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lubunya, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kemalism grew out of a century of Ottoman reform. Attempts to stave off the empire’s collapse began chiefly with the 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> reforms; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-ottomans">Young Ottomans</a> of the mid-century tried to build an Ottoman nationalism, with limited democracy, against rising ethnic nationalism, and after their demise under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Abdul Hamid II’s absolutist reign the Young Turks took up their legacy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a> spent his formative years in Hamidian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a> and, in the army, joined the Committee of Union and Progress (CUP), which agitated for constitutional government; he abandoned Ottoman nationalism for Turkish nationalism and adopted a secular outlook.</p><p>Kept at arm’s length from power by rivals such as İsmail Enver and Ahmed Cemal, he could watch the CUP’s successes and failures. His defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> made his military career. After the Ottoman defeat he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>’ planned partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-thrace">Eastern Thrace</a>, a war that was also a revolution: by 1923 his government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> had abolished the Ottoman monarchy and proclaimed a Republic. Historian Şükrü Hanioğlu finds its roots in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideas of the American and French revolutions, 19th-century positivism and republicanism. The politician Yakup Kadri Karaosmanoğlu used the word Kemalism on 28 June 1929 for “the basic principles and values of the Turkish path to modernity”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau.jpg" alt="Jean-Jacques Rousseau" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurice_Quentin_de_La_Tour_-_Portrait_of_Jean-Jacques_Rousseau_-_adjusted.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maurice Quentin de La Tour (Life time: 1788), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Atatürk refused to be dogmatic and described his ideology as based on science and reason. Its six principles, symbolized by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-six-arrows">Six Arrows</a>, are republicanism, populism, nationalism, laicism, statism and reformism. Together they form a kind of Jacobinism, which Atatürk defined as using political despotism to break down the social despotism that, he believed, the bigotry of the ulema had encouraged among the traditionally minded population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republicanism">Republicanism</a> replaced the Ottoman dynasty with popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and elected government under the law: “Sovereignty belongs to the nation unrestrictedly and unconditionally.” Populism rejected class conflict in favour of class collaboration and national unity.</p><p>Laicism, close to French laïcité rather than the passive Anglo-American secularism, sought to banish religion from government and to bring the religious establishment under the state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nationalism">Nationalism</a>, drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>’s Social Contract, aimed to make a nation state from the remnants of the multi-ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, defining the Turkish nation as the people who established the Republic and promoting the motto “How happy is the one who says I am a Turk”; it renounced Pan-Turkism as an impossible goal. Statism had the state regulate the economy and invest where private enterprise would not, and reformism held the reforms irreversible, with no return to systems deemed backward.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reza-shah.jpg" alt="Reza Shah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reza_shah_uniform.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republican People’s Party (CHP), founded by Atatürk on 9 September 1923, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> through a single-party period that lasted until 1950. The 1924 constitution called Turkey “republican, nationalist, attached to the people, interventionist, secular, and revolutionary”, and on 5 February 1937 the six principles were written into the constitution as state ideology. The CHP did not redefine its Kemalism between the 1940s and the 1960s; from the 1960s it moved toward the centre-left, and in the 1970s, amid a wider rejection of Kemalism in Turkish society, it adopted programmes labelled “democratic left”. The constitutions that followed the military coups of 1960 and 1980 kept its principles of secularism, nationalism and democracy.</p><p>Abroad, Turkey’s transformation was closely observed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and scholars have traced how Kemalism travelled as a practical model through interwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, Albania, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. King Amanullah of Afghanistan, in 1928 the first head of state to visit the Republic officially, drew on it; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> accelerated his reforms after visiting Turkey in 1934, imposing Western dress for men and restricting the veil; and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habib-bourguiba">Habib Bourguiba</a>’s Personal Status Code of 1956 closely mirrored Kemalist family law. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> saw the Kemalists as allies against Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In Atatürk’s fifteen years as president, sweeping reforms advanced a secular, republican and unitary state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> was abolished on 3 March 1924, and the office of Shaykh al-Islām gave way to a Presidency of Religious Affairs. In 1926 the mejelle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> codes were replaced by an adapted Swiss Civil Code and a penal code modelled on German and Italian law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders and their lodges were dissolved, the titles of sheikh and dervish abolished, the fez penalized, all schools placed under one state curriculum, and the day of rest moved from Friday to Sunday. On 10 April 1928 the article naming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as the established religion was removed from the constitution. Turks had to adopt surnames and were not allowed to perform the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a>.</p><p>The regime promoted state support of the sciences and gender equality, purged Persian, Arabic and other loanwords from Turkish, and sponsored the Turkish History Thesis, with its ethno-racial ideas of Turkish origins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>; in 1931 it closed the Pan-Turkist Turkish Hearths. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> an era of etatism built infrastructure, power stations and iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industries, while the masses shouldered the burden of capital accumulation. Non-Muslims were often treated as second-class citizens, and the identity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> was denied for decades, with Kurds called “Mountain Turks”. In 1982, after the coup of 1980 by the Kemalist-leaning military, the constitution was amended to bar Islamic coverings from higher education.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk.jpg" alt="Mustafa Kemal Atatürk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ataturk1930s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kemalism’s control of religion drew criticism from religious conservatives, who argued that a secular state could not control the activities of religious institutions; the policy was nonetheless adopted officially in the 1961 constitution. Its restrictions on personal choice reached religious duty and naming, and the ban on women’s Islamic coverings in higher education was publicly criticized by Joost Lagendijk, a member of the European Parliament who chaired its joint committee with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, though the European Court of Human Rights has ruled in numerous cases that such restrictions do not violate human rights. In 2005 Article 301 of the penal code made it a crime to insult Turkishness; under pressure from the EU it was changed in 2008 to protect the “Turkish nation”.</p><p>Its nationalism has been criticized for its treatment of minorities. Kemalists saw non-Muslims as only nominal citizens, and in 1930 Atatürk called the names of citizens who thought of themselves as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a>, Laz or Bosnians “erroneous appellations”. Like the CUP, it can be said to have endorsed social Darwinism in some way, in wanting Turkish youth healthy and physically strong. Şükrü Hanioğlu calls it a “scientifically sanctioned version of Turkish nationalism” centred on Atatürk, who ruled as an enlightened autocrat bringing top-down change, and it rejected Western emancipatory ideologies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> and socialism. The Nazis viewed Kemalist Turkey as a “postgenocidal paradise” worthy of emulation, and Hitler called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a> the “star in the darkness”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kemalism remains the founding ideology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a>. Its principles stand in the constitution, which since 1982 declares the Republic “loyal to the nationalism of Atatürk”, and its laicism holds that politicians may not claim to protect any religion or sect, a claim that is legal ground for the permanent banning of a party. Article 66 of the constitution carries the Kemalist notion of nationality: every citizen is recognized as a Turk, regardless of ethnicity, belief and gender. By the early 21st century most Kemalists, within the CHP or outside it, still believed in the original six principles, while others criticized its statist tendencies and sought to reduce them.</p><p>Scholarly interest in the transnational history of Kemalism has grown, from the interwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to the Jewish community of British-ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and the Muslim world further east, though most of that work, written in the countries concerned, views it positively and offers few critical insights. Scholars argue that Kemalism became a transnational political category through the circulations of the post-Ottoman space, carried by government and also by material culture, ideas of gender and scientific debate. Those who view it positively link its secularism to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. It endures in the symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-six-arrows">Six Arrows</a> and in the motto “How happy is the one who says I am a Turk”.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Kemalism is the ideology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> in 1923, built on six principles: republicanism, populism, nationalism, laicism, statism and reformism. Its reforms abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> and Islamic law and remade Turkish society on Western models, inspiring modernizers abroad, while its nationalism long denied Kurdish identity. It remains the Republic’s founding creed.</em></p>

